/-!
# Normal generation, perfectness, and simplicity of rank-one unitary groups

Let `K` be a field with an involutive endomorphism `σ : K →+* K`, let `Q` be the three-dimensional
signed reversal matrix `TauCeti.typeAGraphConjugator 2 K`, and let
`G = Matrix.specialIsometryGroup Q σ` be the special isometry group of the `σ`-sesquilinear form of
Gram matrix `Q`. Building on the Bruhat decomposition and rank-one Tits system of
`TauCeti.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.SpecialLinearGroup.Isometry.RankOne.Bruhat`, this file establishes
sufficient field-theoretic conditions under which `G` is normally generated by its
upper-unitriangular unipotent subgroup `U`, the normal core of its Borel subgroup `B` equals the
centre `Z(G)`, `G` is perfect, and `G ⧸ Z(G)` and `TauCeti.DerivedCentralQuotient G` are simple
groups. These conditions hold whenever `σ ≠ id` and some nonzero `a ∈ K` satisfies `a ^ 2 ≠ σ a`,
as well as in characteristic `2` whenever `|K| ≥ 4`:

* **Normal generation (`normalClosure_unipotentSubgroup_eq_top`) and Borel normal core
  (`normalCore_borelSubgroup_eq_center`).** Over any commutative ring `R`, the rank-one matrix
  identity `weyl_mul_torus_eq` expresses `w · torus(z)` as a product of two elements of `U` and one
  `w`-conjugate of `U` whenever `z ∈ Rˣ` satisfies `z + σ z = x * σ x`. Over a field `K`, assuming
  a nonzero skew-trace element `d ≠ 0` with `d + σ d = 0` (which holds whenever `σ ≠ id` or
  `char K = 2`, while excluding all characteristic-`≠ 2` cases with `σ = id`, including
  `SO₃(𝔽₃) ≅ S₄` where `normalClosure U = A₄`), scaling `d` by `σ`-fixed units puts `torus(s)` into
  `normalClosure U` for every `σ`-fixed `s ∈ Kˣ`, and for any `t ∈ Kˣ` with `σ t ≠ t` the
  skew-trace adjustment `s = d * (t - σ t)` makes `s * d * t` have trace `s²`, putting every torus
  element, `w`, `B`, and hence all of `G = B ∪ B w B` into `normalClosure U`. Disjointness of
  `normalCore B` from `U` then forces `normalCore B` to centralize `U`, hence all of `G`, while
  commuting with the central root element `u(0, d)` forces `Z(G) ≤ B`.
* **Commutator generation (`unipotentSubgroup_le_commutator` and `commutator_eq_top`).** Assuming
  some nonzero `a ∈ K` satisfies `a ^ 2 ≠ σ a` (which excludes `SU₃(2)` of order `216`, where
  `σ a = a ^ 2` on `𝔽₄ˣ`), either some `y ∈ K` has `y + σ y ≠ 0` — so `K^σ`-linearity makes the
  trace `t ↦ t + σ t` surjective onto `{s | σ s = s}`, root-group commutators produce every central
  root element `u(0, z₀)` with `z₀ + σ z₀ = 0`, and torus commutators `⁅torus(a), u(x₁, z₁)⁆`
  produce every first coordinate `x` — or else `y + σ y = 0` identically, forcing `2 = 0` and
  `σ = id`, in which case `U` lies on the central `z`-axis and `⁅torus(a), u(0, z₁)⁆ =
  u(0, (a ^ 2 + 1) z₁)` spans `U`. In both cases `U ≤ ⁅G, G⁆`.
* **Simplicity (`isSimpleGroup_quotient_center` and `isSimpleGroup_derivedCentralQuotient`), and
  finite-field specialization (`commutator_eq_top_of_natCard_eq_sq` and
  `isSimpleGroup_quotient_center_of_natCard_eq_sq`).** Combining Tits's simplicity theorem
  `TauCeti.TitsSystem.isSimpleGroup_quotient_normalCore` for the irreducible rank-one Tits system
  on `G` with nilpotency of `U`, normal generation, perfectness, and `normalCore B = Z(G)` shows
  that `G ⧸ Z(G)` and `DerivedCentralQuotient G` are simple groups. When `Nat.card K = q ^ 2` with
  `3 ≤ q` and `σ a = a ^ q`, cyclicity of `Kˣ` supplies elements with `b ≠ b ^ q` and
  `a ^ 2 ≠ a ^ q`, so `SU₃(q)` is perfect and `PSU₃(q)` is simple.

## Main results

* `TauCeti.UnitaryRankOne.weyl_mul_torus_eq`: the rank-one identity expressing `w * torus(z)` in
  `U * (w U w) * U` over a commutative ring whenever `z : Rˣ` satisfies `z + σ z = x * σ x`.
* `TauCeti.UnitaryRankOne.normalClosure_unipotentSubgroup_eq_top`: the conjugates of `U` generate
  `G` whenever `∃ d ≠ 0, d + σ d = 0`.
* `TauCeti.UnitaryRankOne.normalCore_borelSubgroup_eq_center`: the normal core of `B` is `Z(G)`
  whenever `∃ d ≠ 0, d + σ d = 0`.
* `TauCeti.UnitaryRankOne.unipotentSubgroup_le_commutator`: `U ≤ commutator G` whenever
  `∃ a ≠ 0, a ^ 2 ≠ σ a`.
* `TauCeti.UnitaryRankOne.commutator_eq_top`: `G` is perfect under `∃ d ≠ 0, d + σ d = 0` and
  `∃ a ≠ 0, a ^ 2 ≠ σ a`.
* `TauCeti.UnitaryRankOne.isSimpleGroup_quotient_center` and
  `TauCeti.UnitaryRankOne.isSimpleGroup_derivedCentralQuotient`: `G ⧸ Z(G)` and
  `DerivedCentralQuotient G` are simple groups.
* `TauCeti.UnitaryRankOne.commutator_eq_top_of_natCard_eq_sq` and
  `TauCeti.UnitaryRankOne.isSimpleGroup_quotient_center_of_natCard_eq_sq`: over any field of
  cardinality `q ^ 2` with `3 ≤ q` and `σ a = a ^ q`, `G` is perfect and `G ⧸ Z(G)` is simple.

## References

* R. W. Carter, *Simple Groups of Lie Type*, Wiley (1972), §§11.1, 13.3, and 14.1.
* D. E. Taylor, *The Geometry of the Classical Groups*, Heldermann (1992), Chapter 10,
  Theorem 10.22.
* R. Wilson, *The Finite Simple Groups*, Springer (2009), §3.6.2.
-/

